 Description 
 The Space Safety Programme aims to establish European capabilities and services in the domains of Planetary Defence, Space Weather, and Active Debris Removal [ADR] and In-Orbit Servicing [IOS]. One of the ways it achieves this goal is by carrying out various space missions, some of which are handled by the Mission Implementation Division when in Phase B2/C/D/E.
 As part of the Space Weather domain, for example, ESA plans to implement a four-satellite constellation called “Aurora” for monitoring continuously and in real-time the auroral oval, as a key means of enhancing space weather nowcasting and forecasting capabilities in Europe. A first step consists of a single demonstrator satellite mission (“Aurora-D”).
 The ever-increasing number of satellites in orbit poses a significant risk to operational spacecraft and the sustainability of space activities. The ADRIOS (Active Debris Removal and In-Orbit Servicing) missions aim to develop and flight-prove capabilities for rendezvous, inspection, capture, life extension, relocation, de-orbiting and repair of satellites.
 The Mission Implementation team works very closely with the Mission Study team in the CleanSpace and Circular Economy Office in charge of Phase A/B1.
 Space Safety missions often involve companies that are relative newcomers to the space field with ambitions for the private market, which requires the ESA project teams to provide specific expertise and to follow a pragmatic approach.
 Your role will provide you with an opportunity to contribute to pioneering space technologies in-orbit, enabling future safe and sustainable orbital operations.
 Duties 
 As Space Safety Functional and Validation Architect, you will be responsible, from Phase A to Phase E1, for the definition and validation of several complete Space Safety missions including GNC, AOCS and robotics in the loop when applicable. You may contribute in paralle to several missions at different development stages.
 You will fulfil a cross-cutting role within the Space Safety Mission Implementation Division, between projects and also between the Mission Implementation teams and the Mission Study teams from the CleanSpace and Circular Economy Office. You will report to the Project Manager(s) and you will work in close collaboration with the System Technical Lead(s) and, for certain cross-cutting tasks, to the Division Head directly.
 Your key responsibilities will include the following:
 Support Phase A/B1 studies and interface with the mission study lead(s) in the Mission Study team in order to ensure a smooth and efficient transition to the implementation phases:
 reviewing design and interfaces at system and architecture/functional level;
 ensuring that proper verification/validation logic and acceptance methods are proposed;
 helping establish the industrial organisation, the cost at completion, and the project risks and schedule;
 making ad hoc contributions to critical technology development activities to ensure smooth integration in later phases.
 For Phase B2/C/D/E , support the Project Manager(s) with the following:
 Technical execution 
 Define, maintain and coordinate the end-to-end AIT/AIV approach, logic and schedule led by industry, including integration flows, verification/validation approaches, environmental test campaigns and facilities;
 Ensure the timely procurement of all space equipment, GSE and facilities;
 Closely monitor the production of the space segment and ground segment;
 Oversee spacecraft assembly and integration activities performed by industry at subsystem and system levels, including mechanical, electrical, and functional integration processes;
 Ensure a proper spacecraft verification/validation campaign, including:
 functional and performance testing;
 environmental testing (vibration, acoustic, thermal-vacuum, EMC);
 system validation, including the end-to-end guidance, navigation and control (GNC) and robotics chain relevant to rendezvous and capture when applicable;
 possible verification/validation activities specific to the customer satellite when applicable;
 Coordinate with the Operations Manager regarding flight operations procedures and satellite databases in relation to system-level tests; support operations planning and execution as needed;
 Procure the launch service for ESA or support industry for the launch service selection when applicable;
 Follow-up the preparation of the spacecraft for delivery to the launch site;
 Participate in the launch campaign activities.
 Management 
 Support the Project Manager(s) for risks identification and mitigation, schedule control and cost control and propose recovery actions when needed;
 Identify and prioritise the points for attention; report regularly to the Project Manager(s);
 Apply pragmatic decision-making processes to balance ideal solutions with project constraints;
